data.reasoningText:
Let me plan the component decomposition for this settings dashboard, then implement it.

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
SettingsDashboard          (page — owns active section state, orchestrates layout)
├── SettingsSidebar        (vertical tab navigation)
├── ProfileSection         (profile editing form)
├── NotificationsSection   (notification toggles and channel muting)
├── SecuritySection        (password, 2FA, sessions)
│   ├── ChangePasswordPanel (expandable password change form)
│   └── ActiveSessionsTable (session list with revoke)
└── AppearanceSection      (theme, font, language, compact mode + preview)


```

I'm debating whether each section should load its own data or if the page should own all the settings upfront. Since each section is independent with its own save cycle and only one displays at a time, having them fetch individually makes sense, but I'll follow the pattern better by having the page component manage all the data loading instead.

```
I'll go with having each section manage its own data loading since they're independent and only one is visible at a time. Now I need to plan the implementation order: start with the models and service interface, then build out each section component, and finally wire them together in the main settings page. The services are already registered in DI, so I just need to create the interface definitions and start implementing the components.
```

csharp
// Services/ISettingsService.cs
public record UserProfile(string Username, string Email, string FullName, string Bio);
public record NotificationSettings(bool EmailNotifications, bool PushNotifications, bool WeeklyDigest);
public record SecuritySettings(DateTime LastPasswordChange, int ActiveSessions);
public record AppearanceSettings(string Theme, string Language);

public interface ISettingsService
{
    Task<UserProfile> GetProfileAsync();
    Task UpdateProfileAsync(UserProfile profile);
    Task<NotificationSettings> GetNotificationsAsync();
    Task UpdateNotificationsAsync(NotificationSettings settings);
    Task ChangePasswordAsync(string currentPassword, string newPassword);
    Task<SecuritySettings> GetSecurityAsync();
    Task<List<ActiveSession>> GetActiveSessionsAsync();
    Task RevokeSessionAsync(string sessionId);
    Task<AppearanceSettings> GetAppearanceAsync();
    Task UpdateAppearanceAsync(AppearanceSettings settings);
}

public record ActiveSession(string SessionId, string DeviceInfo, DateTime LastActive, string IpAddress);
```

Now I'll create the sidebar component that handles navigation between sections, then build each settings section with its own data loading and form handling. Defining the service interface...

Now I'm planning out the component structure—a sidebar for navigation with section switching, a profile section that loads user data and lets them edit their display name, email, and bio with save and discard options.

The notifications section will have toggles for different notification types and a list of channels with mute controls. Security settings will include a password change panel and a table showing active sessions with revoke buttons and confirmation dialogs. The appearance section handles theme selection, font sizing, and language preferences.

Now I'm ready to start implementing all these components. First, I need to add the Services namespace to the imports file, then I'll write out the main Settings page component which uses a sidebar for navigation and switches between different settings sections based on the active selection.

Writing component code... I'll batch create the component files in parallel rather than invoking them one at a time, since I'm confident with Blazor syntax. For the records with init-only properties, I'll use mutable local variables in the components to handle editing state. Now I'm writing out the ProfileSection component... Writing form inputs... Finishing the save method to create a profile object and persist it, then showing a success toast that auto-hides after a few seconds. I'm also adding a discard method that reverts changes back to the last saved profile. Now I'm ready to generate all the component files.

I'm setting up the CSS layout for the settings page with flexbox to arrange the sidebar and content area, then I'll create the service interface and all the necessary components.

I'm mapping out the full file structure I need to create—the main Settings page, sidebar navigation, and individual sections for profile, notifications, security, and appearance settings, each with their own styling files. That's quite a few files to organize, so I should batch them together efficiently.

I'm deciding between embedding styles directly in components versus using CSS isolation files. CSS isolation is the cleaner approach for Blazor, so I'll stick with that. I also need to update the _Imports.razor file to include the Settings namespace so the components are accessible throughout the app.

